          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Goneril\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal declarative directly presents Goneril’s love for Lear as a true emotional state. The surrounding speech amplifies that avowal, but the marked words themselves already have clear truth-conditional avowing force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.97,\"reason\":\"Goneril does not undertake any future action, duty, fidelity, or relational obligation in the focal words. Lear subsequently grants her territory, but that is his response to her profession of love, not a commitment made by her.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"We make thee lady: to thine and Albany’s issue\\nBe this perpetual.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The expression is a composed answer to Lear’s formal request, not a reflex-like affective discharge. Its heightened surrounding rhetoric does not convert the focal assertion into an involuntary exclamation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love, the expression functions as a competitive and persuasive profession in Lear’s public love test, where the declared degree of love determines bounty. Kent later characterizes Lear as yielding to flattery, supporting a moderate residual function of strategic praise or self-advancement.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\"},{\"block_id\":\"later_1\",\"quotation\":\"When power to flattery bows?\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a formal public division of the kingdom, Lear asks his daughters to declare which loves him most and explicitly links their answers to his bounty. Goneril answers first with the marked avowal and an extended sequence of superlative comparisons.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"absent\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression contains no undertaking by Goneril. Lear treats it as a successful answer to his love test and immediately awards Goneril and Albany a territorial share. Later supplied conduct and references to flattery strongly undermine the claimed affection, though they do not alter the utterance’s original avowing form.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"I recognize the source as William Shakespeare’s King Lear, specifically the opening love-test scene; the title and attribution are also supplied in prior_1.\",\"limitations\":\"The classification concerns only Goneril’s marked occurrence of “I love you,” not Cordelia’s or Regan’s neighbouring declarations. The later evidence strongly supports interpreting Goneril’s profession as strategic flattery and contradicts its sincerity, but the focal words alone do not explicitly state her motive or promise future conduct.\"}"
